NOTES AND QUESTIONS ON MUELLER I
1. The judge denied summary judgment for Taylor Swift and her co-defendants on David Mueller’s claims of interference with his employment at radio station KYGO. Why? Did the judge believe Mueller’s account of what happened? Did the judge disbelieve Swift’s account? Or are these questions irrelevant to why the judge denied summary judgment on these counts?

2. All of the plaintiff’s claims arise out of the same event: Taylor Swift’s accusation that he had inappropriately grabbed her buttocks. Yet Judge Martínez granted summary judgment for the defendants on the slander claims despite denying summary judgment on all other claims. What was different about the slander claims? To be more precise: what was different about the slander claims that made them particularly well suited for resolution through summary judgment?

INTRODUCTORY NOTE ON THE CELOTEX TRILOGY
Anderson v. Liberty Lobby, Celotex v. Catrett, and Matsushita v. Zenith, all decisions glossing the standards for summary judgment and all decided within three months in 1986, are commonly referred to as the Celotex trilogy. Still widely cited, they were the Twiqbal of their day, intensely controversial and perceived by some contemporary legal scholars to effect—or at least reflect—a seismic change in the law.

The core of the standard for summary judgment appears in Rule 56(a): “The court shall grant summary judgment if the movant shows that there is no genuine dispute as to any material fact and the movant is entitled to judgment as a matter of law.” This language poses key definitional questions: What makes a fact “material”? What makes a dispute “genuine”? When can the party opposing summary judgment avoid “judgment as a matter of law”? Anderson v. Liberty Lobby, Inc., 477 U.S. 242 (1986), attempts to answer these questions:

By its very terms, [Rule 56] provides that the mere existence of some alleged factual dispute between the parties will not defeat an otherwise properly supported motion for summary judgment; the requirement is that there be no genuine issue of material fact.

579
As to materiality, the substantive law will identify which facts are material. Only disputes over facts that might affect the outcome of the suit under the governing law will properly preclude the entry of summary judgment. Factual disputes that are irrelevant or unnecessary will not be counted. * * *

* * * [S]ummary judgment will not lie if the dispute about a material fact is “genuine,” that is, if the evidence is such that a reasonable jury could return a verdict for the nonmoving party.

The Court emphasizes that summary judgment avoids the time and expense of trial when the outcome is a foregone conclusion:

The inquiry performed is the threshold inquiry of determining whether there is the need for a trial—whether, in other words, there are any genuine factual issues that properly can be resolved only by a finder of fact because they may reasonably be resolved in favor of either party.

Petitioners suggest, and we agree, that this standard mirrors the standard for a directed verdict under Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 50(a), which is that the trial judge must direct a verdict if, under the governing law, there can be but one reasonable conclusion as to the verdict. If reasonable minds could differ as to the import of the evidence, however, a verdict should not be directed.

On the other hand, summary judgment cannot substitute for trial when the outcome depends on which witnesses a jury will believe or which reasonable inferences from the evidence a jury will draw:

Our holding * * * by no means authorizes trial on affidavits. Credibility determinations, the weighing of the evidence, and the drawing of legitimate inferences from the facts are jury functions, not those of a judge, whether he is ruling on a motion for summary judgment or for a directed verdict. The evidence of the non-movant is to be believed, and all justifiable inferences are to be drawn in his favor.

The Court distinguished between a jury weighing evidence and a jury simply disbelieving evidence. If the parties present contradictory evidence, it is for the jury, not the court, to credit one side’s evidence over the other’s. But
