a non-moving cannot avoid summary judgment merely by arguing that a jury could disbelieve uncontradicted evidence of the moving party.

Justice Brennan dissented. He wondered whether the Court was endorsing the use of summary judgment to abbreviate the judicial process, or whether the Court was unwittingly transforming summary judgment into a procedure that was anything but “summary”:

I am fearful that this new rule—for this surely would be a brand new procedure—will transform what is meant to provide an expedited “summary” procedure into a full-blown paper trial on the merits. It is hard for me to imagine that a responsible counsel * * * will risk either moving for or responding to a summary judgment motion without coming forth with all of the evidence he can muster in support of his client’s case.

Anderson v. Liberty Lobby was a libel case between a self-styled “citizen’s lobby” organized as a non-profit corporation and the editor of a magazine that published allegedly defamatory statements about the corporation. Importantly, though, nothing in the pronouncements quoted above depends in any way on the substance of the parties’ claims against each other: they are generally applicable principles.

That is not say, however, that substantive law cannot affect the proper application of the summary judgment. As Anderson itself notes, “the substantive law will identify which facts are material.” 477 U.S. at 248. In tort, for example, evidence of the defendant’s carelessness will be material to a negligence claim but not to a strict liability claim.

More subtly, though, substantive law can place limits on what counts as “justifiable inferences” that a jury can draw from the evidence. Another case in the trilogy, Matsushita Electric Industrial Co., Ltd. v. Zenith Radio Corp., 475 U.S. 574 (1986), addresses such a circumstance. The plaintiffs, American television manufacturers (including Zenith), sued twenty-one Japanese television manufacturers (including Matsushita). The claim was a conspiracy in violation of the Sherman Act and other antitrust law. Unlike most antitrust claims, which allege that the defendants conspired to raise prices, the plaintiffs’ claim in Matsushita was that the defendants were engaged in “predatory pricing”: conspiring to charge unusually low prices with the goal of driving the plaintiffs out of the market in hopes of jacking up prices thereafter.

581
Despite a gigantic quantity of discovery, the plaintiffs discovered no direct evidence of agreements between the defendants to fix prices of the televisions they sold in the United States.* After discovery, the defendants moved for summary judgment. In opposing the motion, the plaintiffs pointed to the fact that the defendants’ prices were lower than the plaintiffs’ prices and the defendants were gaining market share in the United States. This evidence was consistent with their theory that defendants had conspired to drive them out of the market as part of a concerted effort to raise prices in the future.

But, the Supreme Court noted, this evidence was equally consistent with a simpler, “more plausible” theory: the defendants were competing vigorously by lowering costs and charging lower prices in order to attract consumers. This is usually why one company charges lower prices than another company—and it’s precisely the kind of conduct the antitrust laws are designed to encourage. Given this, can the plaintiffs avoid summary judgment by arguing that a jury could infer from the evidence that the lower prices were the product of an illegal conspiracy, rather than healthy competition? The Supreme Court said no. It explained that “antitrust law limits the range of permissible inferences from ambiguous evidence in a [Sherman Act] § 1 case. * * * To survive a motion for summary judgment or for a directed verdict, a plaintiff seeking damages for a violation of § 1 must present evidence ‘that tends to exclude the possibility’ that the alleged conspirators acted independently.” 475 U.S. at 588. In other words, evidence of lower prices, without more, provides the finder of fact with no basis to favor plaintiffs’ predatory pricing theory over other, more plausible theories. Thus, the Court concluded, the defendants are entitled to summary judgment.
